The task that appears the most times creates the hardest spacing rule. If its count is `maxCount`, there are `maxCount - 1` gaps, and each gap has `n` cooldown positions. When several tasks share that count, they all fit in the last part of the schedule.

The possible schedule length is:

`(maxCount - 1) * (n + 1) + numberOfMaxTasks`

There may be enough other tasks to fill every idle position. In that case, the answer is just the number of tasks.

For example, with `A A A B B B` and `n = 2`, the frame is `A B _ | A B _ | A B`, which takes 8 intervals.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int leastInterval(vector<char>& tasks, int n) {
        if (tasks.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int frequency[26] = {};
        for (char task : tasks) {
            frequency[task - 'A']++;
        }

        int maxCount = 0;
        for (int count : frequency) {
            maxCount = max(maxCount, count);
        }

        int numberOfMaxTasks = 0;
        for (int count : frequency) {
            if (count == maxCount) {
                numberOfMaxTasks++;
            }
        }

        int frameLength = (maxCount - 1) * (n + 1) + numberOfMaxTasks;
        return max(static_cast<int>(tasks.size()), frameLength);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m)`, where `m` is the number of tasks.

Space complexity: `O(1)`, because the frequency array always has 26 entries.

This one simulates time directly. The heap stores task counts that are ready to run, while the queue stores counts that are cooling down and their next available time.

At every interval, a cooled task is returned to the heap. The most frequent ready task is then run. If no task is ready, the clock still moves forward, which represents an idle interval.

The queue is safe to use in front order because tasks enter cooldown in time order. An empty task list returns 0, and `n = 0` works without a special case.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int leastInterval(vector<char>& tasks, int n) {
        vector<int> frequency(26, 0);
        for (char task : tasks) {
            frequency[task - 'A']++;
        }

        priority_queue<int> ready;
        for (int count : frequency) {
            if (count > 0) {
                ready.push(count);
            }
        }

        queue<pair<int, int>> cooldown;
        int time = 0;

        while (!ready.empty() || !cooldown.empty()) {
            time++;

            if (!cooldown.empty() && cooldown.front().second == time) {
                ready.push(cooldown.front().first);
                cooldown.pop();
            }

            if (!ready.empty()) {
                int remaining = ready.top() - 1;
                ready.pop();

                if (remaining > 0) {
                    cooldown.push({remaining, time + n + 1});
                }
            }
        }

        return time;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m log k)`, where `k` is the number of different task types.

Space complexity: `O(k)` for the heap and cooldown queue.

Only the counts matter here, not the order of the input. The most frequent task creates a frame with repeated gaps. Every task tied with the largest count adds one item to the final row of that frame.

The frame length is compared with `tasks.length`. This second value matters when other tasks fill all gaps, because then the CPU can work continuously and no idle interval is needed.

An edge case is `n = 0`. The frame cannot be longer than the task list in that case, so the method returns the number of tasks naturally.

```java
class Solution {
    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {
        if (tasks.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] counts = new int[26];
        for (char task : tasks) {
            counts[task - 'A']++;
        }

        int maxFrequency = 0;
        for (int count : counts) {
            maxFrequency = Math.max(maxFrequency, count);
        }

        int tiedAtMaximum = 0;
        for (int count : counts) {
            if (count == maxFrequency) {
                tiedAtMaximum++;
            }
        }

        int frameLength = (maxFrequency - 1) * (n + 1) + tiedAtMaximum;
        return Math.max(tasks.length, frameLength);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(m)`, followed by two scans of a fixed-size array.

Space complexity: `O(1)`, using 26 counters.

A max heap keeps the remaining task counts in descending order. For each group of `n + 1` intervals, the code takes as many different task counts as possible. Used counts are reduced and placed back after the group.

If tasks are still left, the unused positions in that group must be idle. The last group is counted only by the number of tasks actually run, so extra idle time is not added at the end.

Dry run for `A A A B B B`, `n = 2`:

- First group: run `A, B`, then one idle interval. Counts become `2, 2`.
- Second group: run `A, B`, then idle again. Counts become `1, 1`.
- Last group: run `A, B`, with no trailing idle interval.
- Total: `3 + 3 + 2 = 8`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {
        int[] frequency = new int[26];
        for (char task : tasks) {
            frequency[task - 'A']++;
        }

        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b - a);
        for (int count : frequency) {
            if (count > 0) {
                maxHeap.offer(count);
            }
        }

        int time = 0;

        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            List<Integer> remaining = new ArrayList<>();
            int slots = n + 1;
            int used = 0;

            while (slots > 0 && !maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
                int count = maxHeap.poll();
                if (count > 1) {
                    remaining.add(count - 1);
                }
                used++;
                slots--;
            }

            for (int count : remaining) {
                maxHeap.offer(count);
            }

            time += maxHeap.isEmpty() ? used : n + 1;
        }

        return time;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(m log k)`, where `m` is the task count and `k` is the number of task types. Since `k` is at most 26, this is effectively linear.

Space complexity: `O(k)` for the heap and temporary list.

## Solution
```java
class Solution {
    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {
        HashMap<Character, Integer> freqMap = new HashMap<>();
        for(char task : tasks){
            freqMap.put(task, freqMap.getOrDefault(task, 0) +1);
        }

        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
        for(Integer value : freqMap.values()){
            maxHeap.add(value);
        }
        int total=0;
        int cycle=n+1;

        while(!maxHeap.isEmpty()){
            int i=0;
            LinkedList<Integer> temp = new LinkedList<>();
            while(i<cycle && !maxHeap.isEmpty()){
                int value = maxHeap.poll();
                value--;
                total++;
                i++;
                if(value>0) temp.add(value);
            }
            for(int value : temp) maxHeap.add(value);
            if(!maxHeap.isEmpty()){
                total += cycle-i;
            }
        }
        return total;       
    }
}
```

